UID:10003232-1794328200-1794331800@southcarolina250.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://southcarolina250.com/wp-content/uploads/
 2026/11/A-Patriot-s-Legacy_CALENDER.jpg
CATEGORIES:Lectures
DESCRIPTION:In recognition of Veteran’s Day in our county’s Bicentenni
 al Year\, come learn about the life and times of General Robert Anderson. 
 As our county’s namesake\, Anderson made a name for himself as a militar
 y leader\, frontiersman\, and statesman during the Revolutionary Era and t
 he days of the Pendleton District.
LOCATION:Anderson County Museum\, 202 East Greenville Street\, Anderson\, 
 SC\, 29621\, United States
URL:https://southcarolina250.com/event/a-patriots-legacy/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:"The Promise: Untold Stories of SC's African American Patriots" Di
 nner Theatre followed by a Revolutionary Conversation
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261110T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261110T200000
UID:10003219-1794333600-1794340800@southcarolina250.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://southcarolina250.com/wp-content/uploads/
 2026/10/The-Promise.jpg
CATEGORIES:Commission Events,Events,Performance
DESCRIPTION:“The Promise: Untold Stories of South Carolina’s African A
 merican Patriots” is rooted in real history of 1700s America\, according
  to one of the writers of the stage production. \nAnnette Dees Grevious\, 
 along with Brandi LaShay\, are writers for the Claflin Theatre Ensemble pr
 oduction highlighting the experiences of six Black men captured by the Bri
 tish in Charleston as they attempt to escape and claim the pensions they w
 ere promised. \nThe play explores themes of freedom\, survival and the ove
 rlooked contributions of African Americans during early-American conflicts
 . Through dramatized storytelling\, the production brings attention to the
  struggles and resilience of enslaved individuals who sought liberation an
 d recognition for their service. \nWhile the play includes dramatic licens
 e to weave together a narrative\, Grevious said one of the most meaningful
  aspects of the production is the connection to real history\, even when d
 ocumentation is limited. \n“Each of these men are real men\, and althoug
 h it may not have been exactly their story\, due to lack of documentation\
 , it was definitely some Black person’s story. And this production gets 
 to share and uplift those that are able to view it\,” Grevious said. \nF
 ollowing the performance\, a Revolutionary Conversation will allow for sma
 ll group and communal sharing about the tough subjects involved in the pla
 y. \n“We wanted to create a safe space after each performance because it
  can be a sensitive subject for viewers\, so being able to allow the audie
 nce to ask questions and get clarity or share knowledge with one another i
 s beneficial\,” she said. \nThe production aims to educate audiences whi
 le honoring the untold stories of African American patriots whose contribu
 tions have often been left out of historical narratives. \nThe production 
 was sponsored in part by the Orangeburg County Chamber of Commerce and a g
 rant from SC250. This performance of the play is part of the SC American R
 evolution Sestercentennial Commission (SC250)’s Q2 Commission Meeting ev
 ents. \nThe dinner theater and conversation are free and open to the publi
 c. However\, reservations are required no later than November 2\, 2026.
LOCATION:Claflin University Student Center\, 1810 Magnolia St.\, Orangebur
 g\, SC\, 29115\, United States
URL:https://southcarolina250.com/event/the-promise-untold-stories-of-scs-a
 frican-american-patriots-dinner-theatre-followed-by-a-revolutionary-conver
 sation/
END:VEVENT
